      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "We determined the effect of late-phase responses to allergen challenge of mildly allergic asthmatic patients on the expression and release of the cytokines IL-10 and MIP-1alpha from peripheral blood monocytes."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Sixteen mildly allergic asthmatics were exposed to increasing concentrations of allergen aerosol. Nine demonstrated an early response alone (single responders), while seven had an early followed by a late response (dual responders). Monocytes were isolated from venous blood before and 24 h after allergen challenge, and placed in short-term culture for determination of IL-10 mRNA expression by reverse-transcription polymerase chain reaction and protein release. MIP-1alpha protein release was measured by radioimmunoassay."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "IL-10 mRNA expression was significantly higher in dual responders than early responders alone, for unstimulated monocytes or for monocytes exposed to lipopolysaccharide or IL-1beta. In addition, the release of IL-10 and MIP-1alpha from monocytes of dual responders was also enhanced."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Circulating monocytes are primed to release more of the cytokines, IL-10 and MIP-1alpha, in dual but not in single responders, at 24 h after allergen. They may contribute to allergen-induced inflammatory responses in the airways."
        }
      ]},
